Subject: [PATCH openEuler-26.09 00/25] mm, fs, entry: improve concurrent
 fork/exec scalability

UnixBench shell, execl, and other process-heavy workloads repeatedly
create and tear down dynamically linked processes.  At high concurrency,
many tasks insert and remove VMAs for the same executable, dynamic loader,
and shared libraries.  All of those file VMAs are currently serialized by
one address_space::i_mmap_rwsem and one interval tree.  The lock therefore
becomes a cross-CPU and cross-NUMA bottleneck even though most operations
belong to independent mms.

This series reduces both that shared write-side bottleneck and the fixed
per-exec overhead exposed after it is relieved.  It contains a new i_mmap
batching and sharding implementation in patches 1-19, followed by six
selected upstream hot-path backports in patches 20-25.

Patches 1-3 reduce lock traffic before introducing any new tree layout.
dup_mmap() coalesces up to eight insertions per mapping and keeps four
mapping batches active at once.  The teardown path similarly batches up to
eight removals by address_space rather than struct file.

Patches 4-8 add the sharded layout and route file VMA writers through it.
A contended regular-file mapping gets four cacheline-aligned shards per
possible NUMA node, for up to eight nodes.  Each domain block is allocated
on its NUMA node.  A stable home node selects the domain and a hash of the
mm selects one of its four local shards, providing both inter-node
isolation and intra-node parallelism.

Mappings start on the existing central tree.  After the fourth failed
central write trylock, one writer allocates the full shard set, migrates
the interval-tree nodes under the central write lock, and publishes the
completed layout with release ordering.  Allocation or installation
failure leaves the mapping on the central tree.  shmem, hugetlbfs, DAX,
non-regular files, and systems with more than eight possible NUMA nodes
continue to use the central tree.

Patches 9-17 preserve the less common read-side and global-lock contracts.
Blocking and trylock walkers acquire the complete shard lock set before
the first callback, preventing partial callback execution on trylock
failure.  Caller-locked rmap, take_rmap_locks(), mm_take_all_locks(),
file-folio splitting, memory failure, khugepaged, pagewalk, uprobes, and
the architecture cache walkers are converted to cover every root.  A VMA
count that remains valid during tree migration keeps mapping_mapped() from
observing a false empty window.

Patches 18-19 connect contention-driven installation and make all mmap
optimizations in patches 1-18 opt-in.  CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS is a build-time
capability for MMU, SMP, NUMA kernels.  It defaults to off in Kconfig and is
enabled in the arm64 openeuler_defconfig.  The x86 openeuler_defconfig
keeps it disabled; local x86 testing can select it in .config.  In either
case, the optimized paths remain disabled unless i_mmap_opt=on is passed
at early boot.  The switch is intentionally boot-only, so policy cannot
change while live mappings may already use different tree layouts or
batching semantics.

A default-false static key keeps i_mmap_opt=off on the original mmap
operation path:

  - dup_mmap() performs direct per-VMA insertion under the central lock;
  - exit_mmap() retains its original struct-file batching;
  - shard pointer loads, VMA count atomics, contention accounting,
    allocation, and NUMA state are skipped.

The optional fork batching storage is compiled only with
CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS and is initialized and used only on the enabled path.
No additional dup_mmap() wrapper is introduced; one boot-patched static-key
branch selects between the original and optimized operations.

Patches 20-23 backport the complete upstream entry optimization series in
its original order:

  - commit f955aa8723a6 ("riscv: entry: Convert ret_from_fork() to C")
  - commit 5b3d6103b343 ("riscv: entry: Split ret_from_fork() into user and
    kernel")
  - commit 7ace1602abf2 ("LoongArch: entry: Migrate ret_from_fork() to C")
  - commit e43b8bb56e53 ("entry: Inline syscall_exit_to_user_mode()")

The first three patches move the RISC-V and LoongArch assembly callers to
C before patch 23 removes the out-of-line syscall_exit_to_user_mode()
symbol.  Keeping this order is required for cross-architecture link
correctness.

Patches 24-25 backport two VFS path-lookup optimizations:

  - commit cb80d9074f2a ("fs: optimize acl_permission_check()")
  - commit e631df89cd5d ("fs: speed up path lookup with cheaper handling of
    MAY_EXEC")

The two VFS patches form one unit: the ACL fast path is the prerequisite
for the specialized MAY_EXEC directory traversal path.  A submission
against a tree that already contains these upstream commits should omit
patches 20-25 as the corresponding complete units.

Performance
===========

The supplied UnixBench result compares the baseline kernel with the full
optimized kernel.  The reported values are aggregate index scores:

                         baseline    mmap-opt       change
  1 container              1547.9      2055.5       +32.79%
  80 containers            4201.1      4199.0        -0.05%
  linearity                  36.8%       48.9%       +12.1 pp

The single-container score improves by 32.8%, while the 80-container
score is effectively unchanged and the reported linearity rises by 12.1
percentage points.  The result file contains aggregate values only; it
does not record run count or dispersion, so the 0.05% difference should
not be interpreted as a significant regression.

Validation
==========

The series passes git diff --check and builds the affected fork, mmap,
inode, and memory objects with the sharding capability both present and
compiled out.  A full x86_64 build with CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS enabled
completed successfully.  The affected RISC-V and LoongArch entry/process
objects also compile with Clang 18, and their assembly-to-C symbol pairs
were verified.

QEMU boot and concurrent fork/exec stress tests were run with both
explicit i_mmap_opt=off and i_mmap_opt=on.  The latest full-series test
completed 8,000 concurrent fork/exec operations and the UnixBench spawn
workload without workload-triggered warnings, oopses, or panics.

Scope and trade-offs
====================

Only mappings with observed write-lock contention allocate shards, and a
mapping stays sharded until inode teardown.  This avoids steady-state
memory and multi-root traversal costs for cold files and avoids a reverse
migration protocol.  Once sharded, ordinary VMA writers take one local
shard lock, while operations that require a complete reverse-mapping view
must acquire and visit all shard locks.  The latter paths are deliberately
kept correct rather than optimized for the common writer-heavy workload.

With i_mmap_opt omitted or set to off, patches 1-19 remain dormant and the
original per-VMA mmap operation path is used.  The six upstream backports
in patches 20-25 are independent of that boot-time choice.

